d the mistake gained it nothing. Thirty-four cases were written out in full twice, by the executor before any runner existed and by an outside reviewer without sight of the executor's, and the statement computed on both. Of 679 numbers in the executor's specifications 38 are fixed in value by the cases' words, 118 in order only and 523 are supplied. On nineteen cases from Pettigrew, Kolodny, Carroll et al. and Noggle the statement gives the source's verdict on the act in 15 under the executor's specifications as committed, in 12 once three numbers the reviewer found verdict-directed take its neutral values, and in 12 under the reviewer's; Pettigrew's unhappy parent and Kolodny's just deterrent go against the source under both. The two specifications give another verdict in kind in 8 of 34, five through her informed rating of the course that changes her. None of the seven cases that passed the earlier statement passes, five being caught only by a test for lowered options or knowledge that the executor added; of twelve acts called acceptable the third line flags five or six, each justified by what someone else is owed or entitled to. The twelve formal items hold as stated or with a stated condition; two baselines within the tolerance can give third-line values a whole scale apart, and the inducing test is wrong on two instances. The reviewer gives three cases, accepted by the executor as plain subversion, in which the adviser decides which of two options she rates the same she takes and every line is zero. No verdict differs between the two settings. No Lean; nothing registered<br>MIXED — the join of the account to the corrigibility post is written in Lean, 117 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered: a gate on whole histories made of three predicates and a third-line value, the post's scoring built from it, and the post's Theorems 1 and 3 at that pair. Of the dispatch's six claims: that the post's theorems apply holds by construction and instantiation, the ranges the post asks for being fields of the gate; that a history on which the adviser gained through a mistake of hers scores below every legitimate violation-free history is proved from those ranges, strictly and with no margin; that two legitimate histories with one rating have one evaluation, and that a history on which her information is not sufficient is not legitimate, hold by definition; the bound on what deferring to her verdict costs is proved on a finite space, and holds on a legitimate history because soundness is there defined as a bound on the same miscalibration, with a tolerance; that the third line is never negative, is zero at the baseline and is at least its older form is proved in full, with an instance on which it is 1/5 and the older form is -1/10. The guarantee on what she attains composes with the deferring bound only under a hypothesis linking what she observes to what the adviser knows; on an instance without it every valuation is calibrated in the deferring sense, she has no regret, and she attains 7/20 against a move worth 1/2. Of four further statements: that worsening her options lowers her evaluation, and that the authority module covers control of her act between options she rates the same, cannot be stated with what exists; the post's feedback hypothesis passes from the feedback she gives to the evaluation the join defines under a stated condition and fails on an instance without it; the gate's value is the segment gate's value form, and its predicate is unrelated to the segment gate's. A gate on which every history is legitimate satisfies every theorem: the predicates are given, and nothing connects them to the conduct the post's guarantees are meant to constrain.<br>MIXED — the scoring theorem of the dispatch is in Lean on a finite space, 70 declarations, kernel-checked, none registered, with each of its dependencies a hypothesis of the theorems that use it: the bound on her expected true utility; the bound on the chance that the adviser wronged her, which uses neither of the two assumptions that her evaluation and her verdict do not run above her true utility; the bound on expected detected violations, which uses neither of those nor detection; and the lemma that a course's expected score is at most its audited evaluation on the histories without a wrong. A witness inhabits the whole package with every tolerance positive, and for each of five dependencies an instance has it alone fail and its bound fail. Of the assumptions about her: that her audited evaluation does not run above what she would say shown everything follows, two-sided, from her coherence, authorship and transparency of the actual process to her model, with her evaluation her credence's expectation of a fixed function and her prior the law of the world, and at each thing the adviser knows only if its record carries what it knows. It does not follow from conduct: an adviser whose honest errors fall both ways leaves her evaluation off by a quarter at each thing it believes, and one that acts on the course it estimates higher leaves it an eighth above that course's quality on average, and neither does wrong. That her verdict as she is does not run above her verdict with her valuing unshaped follows from reason mediation only for a course that leaves the inputs to her valuing as the baseline does. With the two assumptions made only where she was not wronged, the welfare bound holds where the penalty times the detection rate is at least one, so the welfare guarantee rests on detecting the adviser's wrongs. Detection, an honest course and the adviser's near-optimality are not derivable; independence of the audit draws and the false-positive rate come from the audit's design. On twenty cases an outside reviewer wrote from the vocabulary alone, the test of responsibility that reads the adviser's gain is wrong on 8 and the one that reads a declared reference on 14. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>MIXED — the account of manipulation as conduct that works through her mistakes is on record as paper proofs with exact searches and four case runs, and none of it is a statement of record. Noggle's definition is kept as the definition of manipulation and what it is for an adviser to be trying to is not formalised. The formal part is a quantity, what the adviser's conduct gains through mistakes it caused in her, through her not knowing what it knows and through an unasked shift in her valuing, and a property, avoidable gain: another option was as good otherwise and had less such gain. For a maximiser over three options, exhaustively on a grid and with no violation: one that takes no avoidable gain has the best value gain aside; what it gives up is at most the extra gain it took; a gate that scores avoidable gain below every other score is maximised without it; removing gain from the reward bounds the loss of that value and does not stop the adviser taking gain. Over many decisions, as an extension of the post's Theorem 3 on paper with the facts at each decision checked exhaustively: authority as in the post; no avoidable gain in the adviser's picture at each decision, from maximisation alone; her informed interest under a second calibration hypothesis; a bound on the frequency of histories illegitimate in fact under a third and a floor on the gate. Each of the two hypotheses, the floor, avoidability judged against permitted options and the range of realised evaluations has an exact instance in which it alone fails; the last was found by the outside reviewer. On cases, each a single coding by language models: three readers' formal reading of Noggle's account gives the verdict they took to be his on 224 of 266 entries and another on 17; an earlier form of the avoidable-gain test agrees with an outside model's verdicts on 37 of its forty cases, and a reader working from the account in words on 39 of the forty as to manipulative or not and on 17 of 28 cases of the book chosen to include every disagreement; on the nineteen value-change cases whose source judges an act the account agrees on 12 and contradicts none, and a variant that makes an unauthorised change of her values a violation of her authority agrees on 12 of the 14 faulted and contradicts 2 of the 5 accepted. Tried and withdrawn in reaching it: seven tests of intent on a single mistake, a definition by an imagined copy of the adviser that gives gain no weight, two readings of the first idealisation, and a rule for valid requests that was circular. Avoidable gain as defined has been computed from a causal model on no case, and no person has checked an encoding. Nothing registered; every component open or a living note.<br>MIXED — the foundation the fifteenth follow-up recorded, legitimacy as the absence of avoidable gain, is set aside, and what replaces it is on record as a statement, a Lean model, exact cases, case runs and nine outside reviews, none of it a statement of record. Mistakes are judged by the adviser's lights: his view says what he owes her and is never where she ought to end up. Legitimacy is three sentences, frozen by the maintainer for testing: nothing untold that matters; nothing done to her behind her back and nothing against her own judgement; she can stop it. What he does to her options, as against to her, is counted under her authority. A formal statement accompanies the sentences and is not frozen. In Lean a model of the adviser's picture of one episode carries the statement as a predicate, with forty theorems, kernel-checked and unregistered: the predicate is unchanged by any bijective re-description of her state; a move that changes nothing in her bears no effect; four refutations, from the disclosure clause, twice from the clause that she can pick out and stop what changes her, and from the clause on her earlier rating; one open, stoppable influence is legitimate and the same influence done covertly is not; the scoring the predicate gives through the gate is the corrigibility result's scoring; and a gate with soundness error ρ and completeness error ρ′ gives legitimate conduct the strictly higher expected score when ρ(1 − w) < −w(1 − ρ′), w the value on failing. The model is simpler than the statement in nine stated respects, and on its one witness only the rule that a move starts after a decision of hers and one clause of four do work. Forty-one exact cases of the formal statement and twenty of a candidate clause for what stopping costs her agree with expectations fixed first; statement and cases are by one hand, and the chance that she goes against her earlier rating is supplied to each case. On the bank of 263 cases from Noggle's book, each run one blind coding by a language model against a scorer written first: the disclosure rule settles 219 of the 240 cases with a definite verdict and agrees on 214, missing none, the rule having been formed with the bank in view; the clause on her earlier rating settles 33 of the 39 cases where a lapse is expected and agrees on all 33, convicting 1 of 69 controls; regulating open pressure, against prohibiting it, was predicted to miss a third or more of the author's manipulation verdicts and missed 5 of the 28 cases it settled; a judgement bent for a while occurs in four cases of 152 as coded and in none where a lapse is expected, so the prediction for it went untested. The statement has not been run on a case of value change; two earlier forms settle about a third of ninety published cases. Nine outside reviews by one model, two of them blind to the project, found errors of form in each version reviewed; none has seen the present statement or the Lean. A ledger of 36 components has 11 closed, each a Lean declaration, 19 open and 6 living notes. Nothing registered. | lean-proved | `prompts/2026-11-10-legitimacy-definition-prosecution/PROMPT.md` | appraisal.mse-split, appraisal.three-channels, appraisal.cost-of-mistakes, appraisal.deferring, appraisal.strong-split, appraisal.guarant